      "id": 1,
      "text": "Labette County, located in Southeast Kansas, was officially founded in 1867 during the post‑Civil War expansion of the state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Labette_County%2C_Kansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The county takes its name from LaBette Creek, the second‑largest creek that winds NNW‑SSE from Parsons to Chetopa.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://labettecounty.com/labette-co-history",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "LaBette Creek itself honors Pierre LaBette, a French‑Canadian fur trapper who settled along the Neosho River and married into the Osage tribe in the 1830s and 1840s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://labettecounty.com/labette-co-history",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Labette County sits in Southeast Kansas, where natural scenery and historic sites blend together.",
